+# Qwen3-TTS on the NPU
+
+Text-to-speech with [Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-CustomVoice](https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-CustomVoice) in FastFlowLM. The talker and the code predictor run on the NPU through FLM's prebuilt `qwen3_npu` engine; text and codec embeddings, heads, sampling and the speech decoder run on the CPU. It serves `POST /v1/audio/speech` (OpenAI / vLLM-Omni shape), a WebSocket endpoint for streaming LLM output into speech, and `flm run` for speaking text to WAV files.
+
+## Validated versions
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| FastFlowLM base commit | `d8767c0a08febd4037356546d2df1bba99bba76e` |
+| `src/lib/xrt/libqwen3_npu.so` | sha256 `1a3ee945bbbdfdecd2a2bfc90a5c5d29e2df8fc4d63f3d67dcc9a83cbc5dd547` |
+| `src/lib/xrt/libq4_npu_eXpress.so` | `58666857753dae8c4e52bebea2063d09fed56971e43b67040f758cc9855dd136` |
+| `src/lib/xrt/libmha.so` | `35b41c0754dff57eefe9c0ddbddfc65151f11769458a4437f4700f2f1973dc81` |
+| `src/lib/xrt/libdequant.so` | `c89ae39e7fe76e7ef8c3e0cdb5141311b6b43dd3b624ec8bb195a6a91e6d6f58` |
+| `src/lib/xrt/libgemm.so` | `4edc9b8ad41059ced21d0b21ce08214fdc784fe4057aa667c10995d1177dad67` |
+| `src/lib/xrt/liblm_head.so` | `ed5d6e230ec184a47922b9ee540e7248160488f9e9efab411cf0e6e8f91fd0f4` |
+| `src/xclbins/Qwen3-1.7B-NPU2/` | attn `88ad573e…`, dequant `575d45e4…`, layer `3f86d2c3…`, mm `4afc2a6f…` |
+| `src/xclbins/Qwen3-0.6B-NPU2/` | attn `88ad573e…`, dequant `575d45e4…`, layer `9e084785…`, mm `4afc2a6f…` |
+| checkpoint | `Qwen/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-CustomVoice` revision `0c0e3051f131929182e2c023b9537f8b1c68adfe` |
+| system | Ryzen AI Max+ 395 (Strix Halo), Ubuntu kernel 7.0.0-38, XRT 2.25.0 (`libxrt2`, `libxrt-npu2`), amdxdna firmware `npu_7.sbin` |
+
+The engine binaries and xclbins are part of the FLM tree at the base commit, so a build from that commit plus these patches uses exactly them.
+
+## Building FLM
+
+```bash
+git clone https://github.com/ROCm/FastFlowLM.git && cd FastFlowLM
+git checkout d8767c0a08febd4037356546d2df1bba99bba76e
+git submodule update --init --recursive
+git am /path/to/patches/*.patch
+cd src && cmake --preset linux-default && cmake --build build -j
+```
+
+Build requirements are FLM's usual ones (Boost incl. program_options, FFmpeg, libcurl, libedit, XRT headers, Rust for tokenizers-cpp) plus a compiler with AVX-512 BF16 support (GCC 12 or newer). Text-to-speech is built on Linux with the XRT runtime only (`FLM_USE_HRX=OFF`); elsewhere `flm` builds without it. CPU-only unit tests for the text segmentation:
+
+```bash
+cmake -S src/test/qwen3_tts -B build-tts-test && cmake --build build-tts-test && build-tts-test/test_tts_text
+```
+
+## Model package
+
+`flm` looks for the package in `$FLM_MODEL_PATH/models/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-CustomVoice-NPU2/` (default `~/.config/flm/models/...`). It is not downloadable; build it with the scripts in this directory (`flm pull qwen3-tts:1.7b` only checks it).
+
+Builds the model package FastFlowLM loads for `Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-CustomVoice`. The talker
+(28-layer, Qwen3-1.7B-shaped) and the code predictor (5-layer, Qwen3-0.6B-shaped) are
+packaged as stock Qwen3 Q4NX models for the prebuilt `qwen3_npu` engine: their embedding
+tables are zero scratch rows (the runtime injects each input embedding) and `lm_head` is an
+identity, so the engine returns final hidden states. Everything else runs on the host.
+The generated weights are build outputs and are not committed.
+
+### Prerequisites
+
+- Linux x86-64, Python 3.12, 24 GB RAM (peak 21 GB: the FP32 model plus calibration statistics).
+- The Python environment in `requirements.txt` (CPU-only torch):
+  ```
+  uv venv --python 3.12 venv && VIRTUAL_ENV=$PWD/venv uv pip install --index-strategy unsafe-best-match -r requirements.txt
+  ```
+  (`pip install -r requirements.txt` works too.)
+- The Hugging Face checkpoint at the revision the package was validated with:
+  ```
+  hf download Qwen/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-CustomVoice --revision 0c0e3051f131929182e2c023b9537f8b1c68adfe --local-dir Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-CustomVoice
+  ```
+  `model.safetensors` sha256 `38b1d5971bdbd982b561cccec982669a53b0537c3cf5e9bd4778ed07bb2f5137`.
+  The build itself downloads nothing (it runs with `HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1`).
+
+### Build
+
+```
+PYTHON=venv/bin/python [CPU_LIST=8-15] ./build_package.sh Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-CustomVoice \
+    $FLM_MODEL_PATH/<model_path>/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-CustomVoice-NPU2 [--threads 8] [--work DIR]
+```
+
+It takes about 1 h 55 min on 8 cores of a Ryzen AI MAX+ 395: trajectories 80 min, official
+calibration runs 23 min, talker quantization 8 min, teacher-forced capture 3 min, everything
+else seconds. Disk: 3.5 GB for the package plus 15 GB of intermediates in `<out>.work`
+(delete it afterwards). The build resumes at the first unfinished step after an interruption
+and ends by printing the sha256 of every package file. With the same thread count, CPU and
+environment, every file is byte-identical between builds.
+
+### Steps and outputs
+
+| step | script | produces |
+|---|---|---|
+| checkpoint | `sha256sum` | `<work>/model.sha256`; warns if it is not the validated revision |
+| config | python | `config.json`: original HF config (speaker and language ids are read from it) plus `flm_version` |
+| tokenizer | `export_tokenizer.py` | `tokenizer.json`: the text tokenizer exactly as qwen_tts loads it, checked against it with the `tokenizers` library |
+| host | `trim_host.py` | `host.safetensors`: every `talker.*` tensor except the transformer layers (BF16) |
+| speech_tokenizer | `cp` | `speech_tokenizer.safetensors`: the 12 Hz codec decoder |
+| predictor | `make_predictor.py` | `Qwen3-0.6B-NPU2/`: code predictor, min/max Q4NX (seconds) |
+| calib_runs | `capture_calib.py` | `<work>/calib_runs`: talker linear-input statistics from 28 official FP32 runs (`calib_runs.json`) |
+| trajectories | `gen_trajectories.py` | `<work>/trajectories`: 150 FP32 code trajectories (50 sentences of `calib_trajectories.json` x seeds 0-2), official pipeline, default sampling |
+| calib_tf | `capture_tf.py` | `<work>/calib_all`: the trajectories teacher-forced through the FP32 talker, added to the statistics |
+| talker | `quantize_talker.py` | `Qwen3-1.7B-NPU2/`: talker, AWQ + GPTQ Q4NX |
+| package_json | `write_package_json.py` | `package.json`: source repo, revision, checkpoint sha256, quantization method, and the garble detector settings from `detector.json` (with their reason and calibration record) |
+
+The engine directories must keep the names `Qwen3-1.7B-NPU2` and `Qwen3-0.6B-NPU2`: the
+engine selects its xclbins by that basename. Their `config.json` files are the stock FastFlowLM
+configs in `configs/` (from the `FastFlowLM/Qwen3-1.7B-NPU2` and `FastFlowLM/Qwen3-0.6B-NPU2`
+model repos) with the layer count and vocabulary set for the TTS sub-models. `q4nx.py` is the
+Q4NX writer shared by both packagers.
+
+The calibration sentences do not overlap the evaluation sentences (`calib_runs.json` rows are
+`[text, speaker, language]`, `calib_trajectories.json` rows `[id, text, speaker, language]`). The official pipeline's
+tokenizer (transformers 4.57.3, `fix_mistral_regex=True`) uses a different pre-tokenizer
+regex from a plain `AutoTokenizer`; it only changes English contractions and case runs, and
+`tokenizer.json` follows the official pipeline.
+
+The package used to validate this integration was quantized from trajectories generated by an earlier C++ FP32 tool; this pipeline generates them with the official Python pipeline instead (same model and sampling). The resulting talker is not byte-identical to the validated one; on the NPU its teacher-forced KL against the FP32 model is 0.073 (validated package 0.069; plain GPTQ 0.079, MSE 0.090), argmax agreement 86.9% (87.5%). On the derail harness (8 sentences x 28 seeds, deployed path: fast dispatch, NPU predictor, repetition penalty 1.05; FP32 model 1.8%) it derails in 4.0% of runs (95% CI 2-7%) against 2.7% (1-6%) for the validated package, a difference of 3 runs in 224 that is within sampling noise; its pitch range is slightly closer to FP32 (8.18 vs 8.19 semitones; validated 7.92).
+
+## Running
+
+```bash
+# speak lines typed at the prompt to flm_tts_000.wav, flm_tts_001.wav, ... (/voice, /lang, /voices, /bye)
+flm run qwen3-tts:1.7b
+# speak a text file to story.txt.wav
+flm run qwen3-tts:1.7b -i story.txt
+
+# text-to-speech server only
+flm serve qwen3-tts:1.7b
+# or next to a chat model
+flm serve qwen3:8b --tts qwen3-tts:1.7b
+```
+
+Loading takes about 6 s (both NPU engines, every speaker's voice prefix, one warm-up utterance). Measured on the validated machine (Ryzen AI Max+ 395) with this build:
+
+| | solo | LLM generating on the iGPU at the same time |
+|---|---|---|
+| time to first audio, from when the first segment's text is available | 59-73 ms | 75-125 ms (memory-bandwidth contention) |
+| generation time per 80 ms frame (mean) | 45-50 ms | 47-51 ms |
+| playback underruns (WebSocket, LLM output paced on `lead`) | | 0 in 7 replies, 26-118 s each |
+
+TTS runs on its own threads, pinned by default to CPUs 0-7 (host heads, embeddings, generator) and 8-11 (speech decoder). Environment overrides:
+
+| variable | default | |
+|---|---|---|
+| `FLM_TTS_CPUS` | `0-7` | host thread pool; the first CPU runs the generator |
+| `FLM_TTS_DEC_CPUS` | `8-11` | speech decoder thread pool |
+| `FLM_TTS_TEMPERATURE` | `0.9` | sampling temperature (talker and code predictor) |
+| `FLM_TTS_ENTROPY` | `package.json` `detector` | override the garble detector's entropy threshold (nats), for experiments |
+| `FLM_TTS_CALIBRATION_DIR` | unset | calibration mode: checks and retries off, every segment recorded there (see below) |
+| `FLM_TTS_WARMUP` | `1` | `0` skips the warm-up utterance at load |
+| `FLM_TTS_KEEPWARM_S` | `600` | for this long after an utterance, a tiny NPU dispatch every 4 s keeps the NPU from runtime-suspending (resuming adds ~135 ms to the next time to first audio); `0` disables |
+| `FLM_TTS_DEBUG` | unset | `flm run`: print each segment's check result |
+
+Test client (needs `pip install websockets==15.0.1`):
+
+```bash
+python tts_client.py speak "こんにちは。" -o hello.wav                    # HTTP, streamed audio
+python tts_client.py stream --file story.txt -o story.wav                 # WebSocket, text fed in pieces
+LLM_API_KEY=... python tts_client.py llm --llm-url https://host/v1 --llm-model MODEL \
+    --prompt "京都でおすすめの観光地を教えて。" --paced -o reply.wav       # LLM output spoken as it streams
+```
+
+It reports time to first audio, playback underruns, cancel latency (`--cancel-after S`) and the server's metrics.
+
+## API
+
+### `POST /v1/audio/speech`
+
+OpenAI / vLLM-Omni request shape:
+
+```bash
+curl -s http://127.0.0.1:52625/v1/audio/speech -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
+  -d '{"input": "こんにちは。今日はいい天気ですね。", "voice": "ono_anna", "response_format": "wav"}' -o hello.wav
+```
+
+| field | | |
+|---|---|---|
+| `input` | required | text to speak |
+| `voice` | `ono_anna` | speaker: `vivian`, `serena`, `uncle_fu`, `dylan`, `eric`, `ryan`, `aiden`, `ono_anna`, `sohee` |
+| `language` | the speaker's own language | `japanese`, `english`, `chinese`, `korean`, ..., or `auto` |
+| `response_format` | `wav` | `wav` or `pcm` (16-bit little-endian, 24 kHz, mono) |
+| `stream_format` | none (whole file) | as OpenAI: `audio` streams raw WAV/PCM bytes as they are generated; `sse` streams `data: {"type": "speech.audio.delta", "audio": "<base64>"}` events (base64 of the next bytes in `response_format`; with `wav` the first one starts with the header), then `data: {"type": "speech.audio.done", "usage": {"input_tokens", "output_tokens", "total_tokens"}}` (text tokens in, 12.5 Hz audio tokens out) |
+| `stream` | `false` | `true` without `stream_format`: `sse` (vLLM-Omni) |
+| `seed` | random | sampling seed |
+| `temperature` | `0.9` | |
+| `replace` | `false` | cancel the utterance being spoken and speak this one instead |
+| `model` | | optional; must be the loaded TTS model (or `tts-1`) |
+
+`speed` (only 1.0), `sample_rate` (only 24000) and `task_type` (only `CustomVoice`) are accepted for compatibility. `instructions` are refused (this build has no instruction-tuned tones).
+
+An error before any audio is sent is a plain HTTP error; once an SSE stream has started, a failure or a `replace` by another request ends it with `data: {"type": "error", "error": {...}}` instead of `speech.audio.done`.
+
+Responses carry `x-audio-sample-rate` and `x-audio-channels`; a whole-file response also carries `x-flm-tts-metrics` (JSON: audio seconds, time to first audio, segments, retries, frame timing).
+
+### `GET /v1/audio/speech/stream` (WebSocket)
+
+For speaking text while it is still being produced, e.g. LLM output. Client to server (JSON text frames):
+
+| message | |
+|---|---|
+| `{"type": "session.config", "voice", "language", "seed", "temperature", "replace", "events"}` | optional; sticky for later utterances |
+| `{"type": "input.text", "text": "..."}` | more text; the first one starts an utterance |
+| `{"type": "input.done"}` | no more text for this utterance |
+| `{"type": "input.cancel"}` | stop the current utterance now |
+| `{"type": "session.close"}` | |
+
+Server to client:
+
+| message | |
+|---|---|
+| `{"type": "audio.start", "utterance_index", "format": "pcm", "sample_rate": 24000, "channels": 1}` | before the first audio |
+| binary frames | 16-bit little-endian mono PCM |
+| `{"type": "lead", "lead": n, "consumed": c, "segment": k}` | text tokens received but not yet spoken |
+| `{"type": "segment", ...}` | per segment attempt: text, frames, check result, retry |
+| `{"type": "audio.done", "utterance_index", "total_bytes", "error", "status", "metrics"}` | `status`: `ok`, `cancelled`, `error` or `npu_fault` |
+| `{"type": "session.done", "utterance_index"}` | |
+| `{"type": "error", "message"}` | |
+
+Text supply. The talker reads 12.5 text tokens per second of speech. When it runs out of text before `input.done` it waits (no padding, which would change the speech), and a long wait can be heard as a gap. Keep the lead at 8 tokens or more. A still-open segment does not start until 8 tokens are available. An LLM generates several times faster than speech, so the simplest client streams the whole reply; a client that paces the LLM should ask for more text when `lead` falls below 8.
+
+### Other endpoints
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| `GET /v1/audio/voices` | voices, languages, default voice, sample rate |
+| `POST /v1/audio/speech/cancel` | stop the utterance being spoken (HTTP or WebSocket) |
+
+### One utterance at a time
+
+While an utterance is being spoken, a new request (HTTP or WebSocket) is refused with 409, unless it sets `"replace": true`, which cancels the current one first. Cancelling (`input.cancel`, `/v1/audio/speech/cancel`, or closing the connection) stops generation within one frame (80 ms of audio). Each utterance holds FLM's NPU lock while it runs, so chat and TTS requests take turns.
+
+## How text is spoken
+
+- Normalization: markdown symbols and list bullets are dropped. A space between two Japanese/Chinese characters becomes 。, because LLMs sometimes separate Japanese sentences with spaces only. Latin and Korean spacing is kept.
+- Segments: text is cut only at sentence ends (。！？… and `.`/`!`/`?` before a space, or a newline), never at commas. Short sentences are merged until a segment has at least 17 text tokens, because very short segments fail about 4 times as often; the last segment of a text may be shorter. A run of more than 200 tokens without a sentence end is cut after its last comma.
+- Each segment is generated from the voice's cached prompt, followed by a pause (350 ms after a sentence end, 150 ms after a comma cut). Its loudness is matched to the first segment's, within ±6 dB.
+- Checks and retries: about one segment in 100 (one per ~25 min of audio) derails into garbled speech. The base model does this too in full precision. Each segment is checked while it is generated: talker code0 entropy over 25 frames, length against the text, and a hard frame cap of 7.5 × tokens + 15. The first segment streams immediately, so a failure there is heard; it is cut where detected and spoken again. Later segments are generated ahead, at most two segments ahead of playback, and checked before they are sent. A failed one is regenerated with another seed, up to 2 retries, without being heard. If playback would run dry first, the attempt in progress streams instead.
+
+In measured LLM-paced runs, 82% of later segments (50 of 61) were checked before playback; the rest streamed unchecked because playback would otherwise have run dry.
+
+LLM guidance: use a plain spoken-style system prompt, e.g. 「あなたは親切な日本語のアシスタントです。音声で読み上げられるので、記号や箇条書きを使わず、自然な話し言葉の文章だけで答えてください。」. Avoid prompts that ask for very short replies: one-word sentences are merged by the segmenter, but a reply that is a single short sentence is spoken as one short segment, which is the case that fails most.
+
+## Recalibrating the garble detector
+
+The entropy threshold (1.7 nats) and the other detector settings live in the package's `package.json` under `detector`, copied from `detector.json` when the package is built; `flm` reads them at load. `detector.json` records why: the selection rule, the clean/failed statistics, and the data it came from. The threshold depends on the talker package and the NPU numerics, so recalibrate after changing either (a requantized or fine-tuned talker, e.g. per-tone LoRA, or a new `qwen3_npu` engine build). Uncalibrated, the FP32 model's value (1.36) flags 6.4% of clean NPU segments.
+
+```bash
+FLM_TTS_CALIBRATION_DIR=$PWD/cal flm serve qwen3-tts:1.7b &        # checks off, segments recorded
+python calibrate_entropy.py generate --dir cal --seeds 6           # 32 replies x 6 seeds, segmented as in production
+# stop flm, then
+python calibrate_entropy.py label --dir cal --threads 8            # Whisper CER per segment
+python calibrate_entropy.py fit --dir cal                          # threshold table and pick
+python calibrate_entropy.py write --dir cal --package $FLM_MODEL_PATH/models/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-CustomVoice-NPU2 [--update-default]
+```
+
+`generate` speaks `calib_entropy_texts.json` (32 Japanese LLM replies, not used for quantization), about 3 h of audio for 6 seeds; `label` needs the packaging environment plus the Whisper model and takes about 3 h on 8 threads. The rule (lowest threshold on a 0.1 grid with at most 0.75% false positives on clean segments) reproduces 1.7 from the original calibration data. `write` replaces the package's `detector` block, recording date, data and rates; `--update-default` also rewrites `detector.json` so later package builds carry the new value.
+
+## NPU faults
+
+TTS watches for NPU failures:
+- an XRT error;
+- an NPU step that does not finish within 2 s;
+- an `amdxdna` error/fail/timeout line in the kernel log (followed with `journalctl -k -f`, so the user running flm needs read access to the journal, e.g. membership in `adm` or `systemd-journal`).
+
+When one occurs, TTS stops for good. The current utterance ends with `npu_fault`, later requests get 503, and the reason is logged. The other models in the process are not touched. Recovery is manual:
+
+```bash
+journalctl -k | grep amdxdna                           # what happened
+sudo modprobe -r amdxdna && sudo modprobe amdxdna      # if the NPU does not recover on its own (stop flm first)
+# then restart flm
+```
+
+## Known limitations
+
+- Linux with the XRT runtime only. The integration drives FLM's prebuilt `qwen3_npu` engine through interposed XRT calls; it is validated against the engine libraries and xclbins listed under "Validated versions" and checks the engine's build shape at load (it refuses to start if that changes). Other engine builds are untested.
+- Preset speakers of Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-CustomVoice only: no instructions/tones, voice design or voice cloning.
+- 24 kHz mono 16-bit PCM or WAV only; `speed` 1.0 only.
+- Audible failures remain. About 1 segment in 100 garbles. Later segments are usually caught and regenerated unheard; first segments and segments the detector misses are heard. Expect a few audible glitches per hour of speech.
+- The garble detector's entropy threshold (1.7) was calibrated on the validated talker package (ASR-labeled LLM-reply segments), not on the reproducible one this pipeline builds. A spot check on the reproducible talker (the 224 derail-harness runs, code0 entropy teacher-forced through the NPU talker): 2 of 215 clean runs (0.9%) exceed 1.7 (validated calibration: 0.7%); of the 9 derailed runs, the entropy check flags 4 and the entropy and length checks together flag 8. The harness labels derails by length, code0 loops and leading silence rather than ASR, so this is a consistency check, not a recalibration. Recalibrate with `calibrate_entropy.py` (about 5 h) together with the next talker change (per-tone LoRA).
+- Prosody can jump at segment joins, because each segment is generated independently (pitch jumps about 2× larger than within a segment). The per-tone LoRA work is meant to address this.
+- One utterance at a time; queued chat requests wait for it, and the other way round.
+- Memory: about 4.6 GB resident for a TTS-only `flm serve`.
